+      "text": "# Build or change software\n\nUse this when implementing a feature or changing software behaviour. Routine document editing, account checks, reading code and maintaining this catalogue do not by themselves start a software build. A specific bug investigation may lead here once a repair is chosen. Review and testing remain distinct jobs and can be requested independently.\n\n## Start and keep the work visible\n\nState the north star: what outcome the user is trying to achieve. Read the project instructions and current work before changing files. Keep a work board with each requested item, acceptance evidence and stage; add new feedback as it arrives. Start a saved `software-build` run with `/Users/aidan/Dev/SessionManual/bin/manual-admin start software-build --client codex` (or `claude`). Save decisions and output references; use the existing run when continuing. The procedure guides judgement, not automatic approval.\n\nRead `/Users/aidan/.agents/skills/build/SKILL.md` and `/Users/aidan/.agents/skills/done-gate/SKILL.md`. For an agreed plan, use the existing `/Users/aidan/.claude/bin/build start PLAN --session SESSION_ID`; do not create a second autonomy engine or silently use an ungated mode. Read the complete plan after arming, and use `/Users/aidan/.claude/bin/build checkpoint --session SESSION_ID` after committed slice evidence. Keep that same explicit session ID for checkpoint, digest and resume commands; the tool's inferred default is not necessarily the Codex or Claude chat ID. New requests must remain visible on the work board and be incorporated through the gate's documented amendment flow, not dropped or hidden by a rewritten promise.\n\nKeep the process available at stage boundaries and on resumption. If a current Shaw build profile exists, read and use its actual interface; no such specific profile is verified by this protocol yet. The saved stage instructions provide the immediate reminder mechanism. Use Trigger Hub for supported wake/event subscriptions through `/Users/aidan/Dev/TriggerHub/bin/triggerhub`, reading its README and current `--help` first. Targets are configured aliases, not arbitrary chat IDs. Verify delivery capability and the correct session owner; never route a wake to another chat by guessing. Desktop thread automations must use the host's supported automation tools when the host requires them; record any missing Trigger Hub bridge instead of claiming one exists. Cancel only this job's temporary subscriptions after completion.\n\n## Reuse an existing solution when it fits\n\nBefore designing a common feature, read `/Users/aidan/Dev/SessionManual/docs/REUSABLE-BUILDING-BLOCKS.md` and look for a matching solved problem or component. Check its maintained source, supported platforms, example, test evidence and limits. Record whether you will reuse it, adapt it or build something new, and why. A catalogue entry is not proof of a ready package: the first live-voice entry points to Columbo source; extraction and an independent consumer remain queued. Transcription and chat-interface entries are research leads. Follow the current capability route when actually using a matched service. This discovery step grants no microphone, screen or account permission.\n\n## Implement a small slice\n\nDefine one useful change and observable acceptance conditions. All new builds use red-green TDD: demonstrate the relevant failure, implement the smallest coherent change, and show it passing. For a bug, preserve the failing reproduction. Do not write tests that merely copy the implementation. Keep earlier passing checks unless the change could affect them; broaden tests when evidence warrants it. Review feedback against the north star and iterate in small slices.\n\n## Independent checks, then Understudy\n\nHave separate **Astra** and **Fable** agents inspect and test the actual changed code. Give both the intended outcome, relevant source and exact evidence. Only genuine **blocking faults** block progress: incorrect required behaviour, broken integration, loss of data or a material permission problem. Style preferences and speculative additions go on the board as non-blocking suggestions. Fix demonstrated blockers and rerun affected checks; do not cycle into gold plating. Missing reviewer availability is a disclosed gap, not a fabricated pass or permission to replace an independent review with the author's account.\n\nOnce no blocking faults remain, always test through **Understudy**, including UI behaviour where there is a UI. Read `/Users/aidan/Dev/Understudy/USAGE.md` and `/Users/aidan/Dev/Understudy/skills/understudy-battery/SKILL.md`; choose realistic flows and assertions for the changed controls, persistence and relevant failure cases. A source-only audit does not prove UI execution. Prefer `cli-run`, headless web checks or non-intrusive render checks when they test the real behaviour. An in-memory window is not screen control. Before a test brings windows forward or controls the pointer/keyboard, use the current screen-access protocol and obtain the user's lease with purpose and estimated duration. A build lookup, test plan or Understudy enrolment does not grant screen permission.\n\n## Deliver the current version and review it with the user\n\nRun the done-gate against committed code and retain review/test evidence. A failing gate names unfinished work; repair it rather than manufacturing evidence. Read the **entire** `/Users/aidan/.claude/skills/walkthrough/SKILL.md` before creating or launching a walkthrough. Check that the launched application is the intended latest build, that every named control exists, and that required application launch actions are included in the walkthrough. Distinguish automated testing from the user's review. After feedback, create a fresh delta walkthrough covering addressed failures and newly visible changes; preserve accepted runs and omit unchanged passed behaviour.\n\nRecord the user's feedback on the work board, continue the next agreed small slice, and update the project ledger/handover using their current skills. Do not claim the user's review has happened because automated tests passed.\n\n## Platform branches\n\n- **JUCE device/plugin:** read `/Users/aidan/.agents/skills/juce-best-practises/SKILL.md` and the relevant references; enrol the folder or session in the existing JUCE Virgil domain using `/Users/aidan/Dev/Virgil/skills/domain/SKILL.md`. Prefer adding to existing domains rather than replacing them. Verify the domain store and host support; never invent an enrolment command. Check realtime/thread safety and test audio and UI separately.\n- **Max for Live device:** use the Max4LiveBuilder/Planck project instructions and the `m4l` Virgil domain. Verify Ableton/OSC availability; do not assume an Ableton MCP is installed. Prefer headless/render checks where meaningful; real Ableton control follows the screen protocol.\n- **iOS app:** identify the correct scheme, destination, bundle ID and installed build. Use Understudy's current simulator flow instructions; distinguish simulator automation from tests that take the physical desktop.\n- **macOS app:** verify the exact app bundle and executable being tested. Prefer non-intrusive render tests when they cover the change; visible app interaction needs the screen lease. Check persistence/relaunch against that same build.\n\nIf a tool, domain, wake target or platform capability is missing, record what is unavailable and continue independent work. Do not convert uncertainty about unrelated commands into blanket holds.\n"
